.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt DD 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm dd
.Nd convert and copy a file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar operand ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
copies its input to its output in blocks, converting as asked.
It reads input blocks of
.Cm ibs
bytes, and gathers what it reads into output blocks of
.Cm obs
bytes.
When it is done, or interrupted, it writes a summary on the standard error.
.Pp
The operands are:
.Bl -tag -width "count=n"
.It Cm if Ns = Ns Ar file
Read
.Ar file
instead of the standard input.
.It Cm of Ns = Ns Ar file
Write
.Ar file
instead of the standard output.
It is created if need be, with mode 0666 less the umask, and truncated unless
.Cm seek
or
.Cm conv Ns = Ns Cm notrunc
is given.
.It Cm ibs Ns = Ns Ar n
The input block size (512).
.It Cm obs Ns = Ns Ar n
The output block size (512).
.It Cm bs Ns = Ns Ar n
Both block sizes.
If no conversion is asked for but
.Cm noerror ,
.Cm notrunc
and
.Cm sync ,
each block read is written as one block, however short.
.It Cm cbs Ns = Ns Ar n
The record size for
.Cm block
and
.Cm unblock .
.It Cm skip Ns = Ns Ar n
Pass by
.Ar n
input blocks before copying: by seeking where the input can, else by reading.
.Cm iseek
is the same.
.It Cm seek Ns = Ns Ar n
Seek past
.Ar n
output blocks before writing; a regular output file is cut there unless
.Cm conv Ns = Ns Cm notrunc .
.Cm oseek
is the same.
.It Cm count Ns = Ns Ar n
Copy only
.Ar n
input blocks.
.It Cm conv Ns = Ns Ar value Ns Op , Ns Ar value ...
Conversions, any of:
.Bl -tag -width fdatasync
.It Cm block
Treat the input as lines; write each as a record of
.Cm cbs
bytes, padded with spaces, or cut to fit (counted as truncated).
.It Cm unblock
Treat the input as records of
.Cm cbs
bytes; write each without its trailing spaces and with a newline.
.It Cm lcase , ucase
Map letters to lower or upper case.
.It Cm swab
Swap each pair of input bytes.
.It Cm noerror
Go on after a read error, writing the summary so far; with
.Cm sync
the lost block is written as NULs, without it the block is left out.
.It Cm notrunc
Don't truncate the output file.
.It Cm sync
Pad each input block to
.Cm ibs
bytes: with spaces for
.Cm block
and
.Cm unblock ,
else with NULs.
.It Cm sparse
Seek over output blocks made only of NULs instead of writing them, leaving a
hole in the file.
.It Cm fsync , fdatasync
Flush the output to its device at the end.
.El
.It Cm iflag Ns = Ns Cm fullblock
Fill each input block, reading again after a short read (from a pipe or
terminal).
.It Cm oflag Ns = Ns Ar value Ns Op , Ns Ar value ...
.Cm fsync
as
.Cm conv Ns = Ns Cm fsync ;
.Cm sync
opens the output for synchronous writes.
.It Cm status Ns = Ns Ar value
.Cm noxfer
leaves out the bytes transferred line,
.Cm none
the whole summary;
.Cm progress
also shows a running count about once a second.
.El
.Pp
A number may end in
.Cm b
(512),
.Cm k ,
.Cm m ,
.Cm g ,
.Cm t ,
.Cm p
or
.Cm e
(powers of 1024), or
.Cm w
(4), and numbers may be multiplied with
.Cm x :
.Ql bs=2x512 .
.Pp
The summary reads
.Bd -literal -offset indent
f+p records in
f+p records out
n bytes transferred in s secs (r bytes/sec)
.Ed
.Pp
where
.Ar f
counts full blocks and
.Ar p
partial ones, followed by how many blocks
.Cm swab
found of odd length and how many records
.Cm block
cut short, when there were any.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
After an interrupt
.Nm
writes the summary and dies of the signal.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Write an image to a disk:
.Dl # dd if=oxidebsd.img of=/dev/vtbd1 bs=1m conv=fsync
.Pp
Make a 64 MB file that takes no space:
.Dl $ dd if=/dev/zero of=disk.img bs=1m count=64 conv=sparse
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cp 1 ,
.Xr tr 1
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq),
less the
.Cm ascii ,
.Cm ebcdic
and
.Cm ibm
conversions, which are not provided.
.Cm iseek ,
.Cm oseek ,
.Cm iflag ,
.Cm oflag ,
.Cm status
and the
.Cm sparse ,
.Cm fsync
and
.Cm fdatasync
conversions are extensions, as in the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v5 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
